<!-- doc/src/sgml/pl_lua.sgml -->

<chapter id="pllua">
 <title>PL/Lua &mdash; Lua Procedural Language</title>

 <indexterm zone="pllua">
  <primary>PL/Lua</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <indexterm zone="pllua">
  <primary>Lua</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <para>
  PL/Lua lets you write <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> functions,
  procedures, triggers and anonymous code blocks in the
  <ulink url="https://www.lua.org">Lua</ulink> programming language.
  Lua is a small, fast language designed to be embedded in other programs.
  The language is the 5.4 version of Lua (release 5.4.6 or later), or
  Lua 5.5, whichever PL/Lua was built with.
 </para>

 <para>
  Simple <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> values, such as numbers,
  strings and booleans, become the corresponding Lua values.  Values of
  all other types are represented by <firstterm>datum objects</firstterm>,
  which keep the SQL type of the value and can be indexed, iterated and
  converted to and from Lua tables where that makes sense.
  Lua code reaches the database through the <literal>spi</literal> module,
  described in <xref linkend="pllua-spi"/>.
 </para>

 <note>
  <para>
   PL/Lua is built only if requested when <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>
   is configured; see <xref linkend="installation"/>.  Binary packages
   might provide it as a separate package.
  </para>
 </note>

 <sect1 id="pllua-install">
  <title>Installing PL/Lua</title>

  <indexterm zone="pllua-install">
   <primary>trusted</primary>
   <secondary>PL/Lua</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   PL/Lua provides two languages, installed by two extensions that share
   one library:
<programlisting>
CREATE EXTENSION pl_lua;    -- trusted language pl_lua
CREATE EXTENSION pl_luau;   -- untrusted language pl_luau
</programlisting>
   Either or both can be installed in a database.  If a language is
   installed in <literal>template1</literal>, it is installed in every
   database created afterwards.
  </para>

  <para>
   Code in the trusted language <literal>pl_lua</literal> runs in a
   sandbox (see <xref linkend="pllua-sandbox"/>) that does not provide
   access to the file system, to other programs, or to arbitrary Lua
   modules, so any user who has been granted <literal>USAGE</literal> on
   the language can write functions in it.  Code in the untrusted language
   <literal>pl_luau</literal> has the complete Lua standard library, including
   <literal>io</literal>, <literal>os</literal> and <literal>debug</literal>,
   and can load any module; it can do anything the operating system
   account running the server can do, and so only superusers can create
   functions in it.
  </para>

  <para>
   Each session has its own Lua interpreter state for
   <literal>pl_luau</literal> and one for <literal>pl_lua</literal> for each
   role that runs trusted code.  The role is the current user at the time
   of the call, so a <literal>SECURITY DEFINER</literal> function runs in
   the state belonging to its owner.  Global variables set by one role's
   code are never visible to another role, and the two languages share no
   Lua data.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="pllua-functions">
  <title>Writing Functions</title>

  <para>
   PL/Lua functions are created with the usual
   <xref linkend="sql-createfunction"/> command, giving the body of a Lua
   function as the function body:
<programlisting>
CREATE FUNCTION greet(name text) RETURNS text AS $$
    return "Hello, " .. name .. "!"
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;

SELECT greet('world');
</programlisting>
   Procedures are created with <xref linkend="sql-createprocedure"/>, and
   anonymous blocks of code are run with <xref linkend="sql-do"/>:
<programlisting>
DO $$ print("running on " .. _VERSION) $$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;
</programlisting>
   An anonymous block takes no arguments, and any value it returns is
   discarded.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="pllua-functions-args">
   <title>Arguments and Results</title>

   <para>
    The named arguments of a SQL function are the named parameters of the
    Lua function, so their names must be valid Lua identifiers (and not
    Lua keywords).  If some arguments have no name, the function also takes
    a trailing <literal>...</literal> parameter that receives the arguments
    from the first unnamed one onwards; a named argument may not follow an
    unnamed one.  A <literal>VARIADIC "any"</literal> argument is passed
    as separate Lua values in the same way, and so cannot be named; any
    other variadic argument is passed as one array.
<programlisting>
CREATE FUNCTION add2(a integer, b integer) RETURNS integer AS $$
    return a + b
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;

CREATE FUNCTION mul2(integer, integer) RETURNS integer AS $$
    local a, b = ...
    return a * b
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;
</programlisting>
    Polymorphic arguments and results are supported.  Use
    <literal>pgtype(<replaceable>value</replaceable>, <replaceable>n</replaceable>)</literal>
    (see <xref linkend="pllua-pgtype"/>) to find the actual SQL type of
    argument number <replaceable>n</replaceable>, which is needed when the
    argument arrived as a plain Lua value.
   </para>

   <para>
    A SQL null is passed as <literal>nil</literal>, and a function that
    returns <literal>nil</literal> or returns nothing at all returns null.
    The returned value is converted to the SQL result type by the rules in
    <xref linkend="pllua-values"/>.
   </para>

   <para>
    A function returning a composite type can return a table indexed by
    column name, a row value, or exactly one Lua value for each column; a
    function with <literal>OUT</literal> parameters is treated the same way:
<programlisting>
CREATE TYPE pair AS (a integer, b text[]);

CREATE FUNCTION make_pair() RETURNS pair AS $$
    return { a = 123, b = { "x", "y" } }
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;

CREATE FUNCTION two_values(OUT n integer, OUT s text) AS $$
    return 1, 'one'
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;
</programlisting>
    If fewer values are returned than there are columns, the rest are null;
    returning more is an error.  A procedure's return values, if it has
    <literal>INOUT</literal> or <literal>OUT</literal> parameters, supply
    their values in order; otherwise they are ignored.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-functions-srf">
   <title>Set-Returning Functions</title>

   <para>
    A function declared <literal>RETURNS SETOF</literal> or
    <literal>RETURNS TABLE</literal> runs as a Lua coroutine.  It produces
    each row by passing the value, or for a composite result the values of
    the columns, to <function>coroutine.yield</function>; the set ends when
    the function returns.
<programlisting>
CREATE FUNCTION squares(n integer) RETURNS TABLE (i integer, sq integer) AS $$
    for i = 1, n do
        coroutine.yield(i, i * i)
    end
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;
</programlisting>
    Yielding no values produces a row of nulls.  A function that returns
    values without ever having yielded produces a single row from them, and
    one that returns nothing without yielding produces no rows.
   </para>

   <para>
    The function is resumed once per row, so it interleaves with the rest
    of the query.  If the query stops early, for instance because of a
    <literal>LIMIT</literal>, the coroutine is closed at once, which runs
    the handlers of any pending to-be-closed variables
    (<literal>local x &lt;close&gt;</literal>, including those that generic
    <literal>for</literal> loops use).
   </para>

   <para>
    A set-returning function cannot call <function>coroutine.yield</function>
    from inside a function run by <function>pcall</function> or
    <function>xpcall</function>; that raises the error <quote>attempt to yield
    across a C-call boundary</quote>.  The called function runs in a
    subtransaction, which could not be left open while the query goes on to
    other work.  Collect the rows in the protected function and yield them
    outside it.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-functions-env">
   <title>Function Environments</title>

   <para>
    The first time a function is called in a session, PL/Lua compiles
    the following Lua chunk and runs it, with a fresh environment table
    passed in as <literal>self</literal>:
<programlisting>
local self = (...) local function <replaceable>name</replaceable>(<replaceable>params</replaceable>) <replaceable>body</replaceable>
end return <replaceable>name</replaceable>
</programlisting>
    The body begins on the first line of the chunk, so line numbers in Lua
    error messages match the function body.  The environment table is empty
    but falls back to the global environment (the sandbox, for
    <literal>pl_lua</literal>).  So a variable assigned without
    <literal>local</literal> belongs to that function: it persists between
    calls in the session and is invisible to other functions.  To share data
    between functions, store it in <literal>_G</literal> explicitly.  The
    compiled function is kept until the session ends or the function is
    redefined.
   </para>

   <para>
    Because the body is placed inside a function definition, a function
    can run one-time initialization code by ending the function early and
    opening a <literal>do</literal> block, whose code runs when the function
    is compiled:
<programlisting>
CREATE FUNCTION lookup(id integer) RETURNS text STABLE AS $$
    local r = q:execute(id)
    return r[1] and r[1].value
end
do
    q = spi.prepare("SELECT value FROM objects WHERE id = $1", { "integer" })
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;
</programlisting>
    Anonymous blocks are compiled as a chunk and run directly, with a fresh
    environment, so they have the same variable scoping.
   </para>

   <para>
    When <xref linkend="guc-pl-lua-strict"/> is on while a function is
    compiled, reading a variable that exists in neither the function's
    environment nor the global environment is an error rather than
    <literal>nil</literal>; this catches misspelled names.  Assignment to
    undeclared variables is unaffected.
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="pllua-values">
  <title>Values and Types</title>

  <sect2 id="pllua-values-mapping">
   <title>Conversion Between SQL and Lua</title>

   <para>
    A value passed from SQL to Lua (an argument, a query result column,
    an array element and so on) becomes:
    <itemizedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para><literal>nil</literal>, if it is null.</para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       A Lua string, for <type>text</type>, <type>varchar</type>,
       <type>char</type> (including the padding), <type>name</type>,
       <type>xml</type>, <type>json</type>, <type>cstring</type> and enum
       values.  A <type>bytea</type> value becomes a string of its raw
       bytes, not its escaped text form.
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>A Lua boolean, for <type>boolean</type>.</para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       A Lua integer, for <type>smallint</type>, <type>integer</type>,
       <type>bigint</type> and <type>oid</type>; a Lua float, for
       <type>real</type> and <type>double precision</type>.
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>A cursor object, for <type>refcursor</type>.</para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       The result of the transform for the type, if the function lists the
       type in its <literal>TRANSFORM FOR TYPE</literal> clause and a
       transform exists for the language.
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>A datum object, for anything else.</para>
     </listitem>
    </itemizedlist>
    Values of a domain type are converted as the base type.  Datum objects
    can always be converted to text with <function>tostring</function>.
   </para>

   <para>
    A Lua value converted to a SQL type (a result, a query parameter, an
    array element...) is converted like this:
    <itemizedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para><literal>nil</literal> becomes null, for any type.</para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>A datum object of the right type is used as it is.</para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>A Lua boolean converts to <type>boolean</type>.</para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       A Lua string converts to <type>text</type>, <type>varchar</type>,
       <type>cstring</type> and <type>refcursor</type>, and to
       <type>bytea</type> by copying its bytes unchanged.  It converts to
       <type>boolean</type> only if it is
       <literal>true</literal>, <literal>t</literal>, <literal>1</literal>,
       <literal>false</literal>, <literal>f</literal> or
       <literal>0</literal>.
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       A Lua number converts to <type>smallint</type>,
       <type>integer</type>, <type>bigint</type> and <type>oid</type> (it
       must be an exact integer in range), to <type>real</type>,
       <type>double precision</type> and <type>numeric</type>, and to
       <type>boolean</type> only if it is 0 or 1.
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>A cursor object converts to <type>refcursor</type>.</para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Otherwise a transform applies, if there is one, or the type's own
       conversion for <type>jsonb</type> and the date and time types, or,
       for a string, the type's input function, or, for a table, the
       constructor for the type (see <xref linkend="pllua-pgtype"/>).  If none
       of these works, an error is raised.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </itemizedlist>
   </para>

   <para>
    A transform (see <xref linkend="sql-createtransform"/>) is used only by
    a function that names the type in its <literal>TRANSFORM</literal>
    clause.  It then applies to the function's arguments and result and to
    every other value converted while the function runs, such as query
    parameters, rows returned by <literal>spi</literal>, array elements and
    composite columns.  Composite columns and array elements are converted
    when they are accessed, so a value kept in a global variable and read
    later is converted under the transform list of the function reading it.
    Anonymous code blocks have no <literal>TRANSFORM</literal> clause and use
    no transforms.  For example, with the transform from
    <xref linkend="pllua-hstore"/>:
<programlisting>
CREATE FUNCTION hstore_keys(h hstore) RETURNS text
TRANSFORM FOR TYPE hstore AS $$
    local keys = {}
    for k in pairs(h) do keys[#keys + 1] = k end
    table.sort(keys)
    return table.concat(keys, ',')
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;
</programlisting>
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-pgtype">
   <title>Type Objects and Constructors</title>

   <para>
    The global table <literal>pgtype</literal>, which is also the module
    <literal>pl_lua.pgtype</literal>, provides <firstterm>type
    objects</firstterm> describing SQL types:
    <variablelist>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal>pgtype(<replaceable>value</replaceable>)</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        The type object of a datum object, or <literal>nil</literal> if the
        argument is not a datum object.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal>pgtype(<replaceable>value</replaceable>, <replaceable>n</replaceable>)</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        The type object of a datum object; for any other value, the type
        object of argument <replaceable>n</replaceable> (counting from 1) of
        the running function, or of its result type if
        <replaceable>n</replaceable> is 0.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal>pgtype.<replaceable>name</replaceable></literal>, <literal>pgtype['<replaceable>name</replaceable>']</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        The type object for the SQL type with that name, or
        <literal>nil</literal> if there is none.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal>pgtype.array.<replaceable>name</replaceable></literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        The type object for the array type with that element type.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
   </para>

   <para>
    A type object can be called as a constructor, and has these methods:
    <variablelist>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal><replaceable>t</replaceable>(...)</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        Makes a datum of the type; the arguments depend on the kind of type,
        as listed below.  Given a single datum of another type, it applies the
        assignment cast between the two types if there is one.  As in SQL,
        the current user needs <literal>EXECUTE</literal> privilege on the
        cast function and on any length-coercion function (for example for
        <type>varchar(<replaceable>n</replaceable>)</type> columns).
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal><replaceable>t</replaceable>:fromstring(<replaceable>s</replaceable>)</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>Makes a datum from its text representation.</para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal><replaceable>t</replaceable>:frombinary(<replaceable>s</replaceable>)</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        Makes a datum from its binary (<literal>send</literal>/<literal>receive</literal>)
        representation, which for many types depends on <xref linkend="guc-client-encoding"/>.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal><replaceable>t</replaceable>:name([<replaceable>typmod</replaceable>])</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>The type's name as SQL syntax, as <function>format_type</function> gives it.</para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal><replaceable>t</replaceable>:element([<replaceable>column</replaceable>])</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        The type object of the element type of an array or range type, or of
        the named column of a composite type.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal><replaceable>t</replaceable>:kind()</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        <literal>composite</literal>, <literal>array</literal> or
        <literal>range</literal> for a type of that kind (also for a domain
        over one), and <literal>scalar</literal> for any other type.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
   </para>

   <para>
    The constructor arguments by kind of type are:
    <variablelist>
     <varlistentry>
      <term>Scalar types</term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        One value, converted as in <xref linkend="pllua-values-mapping"/>.
        For some types, notably <type>bytea</type>, this differs from
        <function>fromstring</function>, which parses the text form.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term>Composite types</term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        Either one table (or other indexable value) giving the columns by
        name, or one value for each column in order.  Length coercions such as
        those of <type>varchar(<replaceable>n</replaceable>)</type> columns are applied.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term>Array types</term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        No arguments gives an empty array.  A table followed by one integer for
        each dimension gives an array of those dimensions filled from the table
        by indexing.  A table alone gives a one-dimensional array as long
        as the table's largest integer key.  Any other arguments become the
        elements of a one-dimensional array.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term>Range types</term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        No arguments gives an empty range; one string is parsed as the text
        form; otherwise the arguments are the lower bound, the upper bound
        and optionally a bounds string as in
        <xref linkend="rangetypes-construct"/>.  A <literal>nil</literal> bound
        is infinite.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
<programlisting>
local d = pgtype.date('2026-12-01')
local a = pgtype.array.integer({ { 1, 2 }, { 3, 4 } }, 2, 2)
local r = pgtype.numrange(1, 2, '[]')
local p = pgtype.pair({ a = 1, b = { "x", "y" } })
</programlisting>
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-values-composite">
   <title>Composites, Arrays and Ranges</title>

   <para>
    A composite datum is indexed by column name or attribute number, and
    its columns can be assigned to.  Attribute numbers include dropped
    columns, so they are not always the same as column positions.
    <function>pairs</function> returns the column name, value and attribute
    number of each live column in order; do not use
    <function>ipairs</function>.
<programlisting>
CREATE FUNCTION bump(rec pair) RETURNS pair AS $$
    rec.a = rec.a + 1
    for name, value, attno in pairs(rec) do print(name, value, attno) end
    return rec
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;
</programlisting>
   </para>

   <para>
    An array datum is indexed by number, with several subscripts for
    several dimensions (<literal>a[i][j]</literal>), and its elements can
    be assigned to.  Assigning outside the bounds of a one-dimensional array
    extends it, with nulls in between.  <function>pairs</function> and
    <function>ipairs</function> iterate over it, and <literal>#a</literal> is
    the length of a one-dimensional array.  A range datum has the read-only
    fields <literal>lower</literal>, <literal>upper</literal>,
    <literal>lower_inc</literal>, <literal>upper_inc</literal>,
    <literal>lower_inf</literal>, <literal>upper_inf</literal> and
    <literal>isempty</literal>.
   </para>

   <para>
    Calling a composite, array or <type>jsonb</type> datum converts it to a Lua table.  The
    optional argument is a table of options:
<programlisting>
row{ map = function(colname, value, attno, row) ... end, null = v, discard = bool }
array{ map = function(elem, array, i, j, ...) ... end, null = v, discard = bool }
</programlisting>
    Nulls are replaced by <replaceable>v</replaceable> (default
    <literal>nil</literal>) before <literal>map</literal> is called, and an
    entry for which <literal>map</literal> returns <literal>nil</literal> is
    left out.  The map function of an array receives one index for each
    dimension, and a multidimensional array gives nested tables.  If
    <literal>discard</literal> is true, nothing is returned and the call is
    made only for what the map function does.  A call with a function as its
    argument means <literal>{ map = f }</literal>, and with any other
    non-table argument <literal>{ null = v }</literal>.
<programlisting>
CREATE FUNCTION array_sum(a integer[]) RETURNS integer AS $$
    local total = 0
    a{ null = 0, discard = true,
       map = function(v) total = total + v end }
    return total
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;
</programlisting>
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-values-compare">
   <title>Comparing Datum Objects</title>

   <para>
    Two datum objects of the same SQL type can be compared with
    <literal>==</literal>, <literal>&lt;</literal> and
    <literal>&lt;=</literal> (and so with <literal>&gt;</literal> and
    <literal>&gt;=</literal>), using the ordering of the type's default btree
    operator class.  This works for the date and time types,
    <type>jsonb</type>, <type>numeric</type>, arrays, composites and any other
    type with such an operator class.  Datum objects of different types are
    never equal, and comparing them with <literal>&lt;</literal> raises an
    error.  A datum object is also never equal to a plain Lua value of any
    kind, so convert one side first if you need to compare a datum with, say,
    a Lua string or number.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-numeric">
   <title>Numeric Values</title>

   <para>
    <type>numeric</type> values are datum objects that support the Lua
    arithmetic and comparison operators with <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>'s
    exact decimal arithmetic.  An operation between a numeric and a Lua
    number gives a numeric.  The <literal>//</literal> operator truncates
    toward zero and <literal>%</literal> takes the sign of the dividend, as in
    SQL, which differs from Lua's own behavior for numbers.  A numeric never
    equals a Lua number with <literal>==</literal>, though two numerics with the
    same value are equal; so numerics are not useful as table keys.
   </para>

   <para>
    These functions are methods of numeric values and are also in the module
    <literal>pl_lua.numeric</literal>, where they also accept Lua numbers:
    <function>abs</function>, <function>ceil</function>,
    <function>equal</function>, <function>exp</function>,
    <function>floor</function>, <function>isnan</function>,
    <function>sign</function>, <function>sqrt</function>,
    <function>log</function> (with an optional base),
    <function>round</function> and <function>trunc</function> (with an
    optional number of decimal places), <function>tointeger</function>
    (<literal>nil</literal> unless the value is exactly a Lua integer) and
    <function>tonumber</function> (an approximate float).  The module's
    <function>new</function> function, like <literal>pgtype.numeric</literal>,
    makes a numeric.
<programlisting>
local num = require 'pl_lua.numeric'
local x = pgtype.numeric('1.10') * 3
print(x, x:round(1), num.equal(x, 3.3))
</programlisting>
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-jsonb">
   <title>JSON Values</title>

   <para>
    <type>json</type> values are passed as strings.  <type>jsonb</type>
    values are datum objects, iterated with <function>pairs</function>
    and converted to tables by calling them with options:
<programlisting>
jsonbval{ map = function(key, value, ...) ... end, null = v, discard = bool,
          pg_numeric = bool, norecurse = bool }
</programlisting>
    The map function receives the key (an index for an array element, a
    string for an object member, or <literal>nil</literal> for a top-level
    scalar), the value, and then the keys leading to the current container.
    The key and value it returns are stored in the result.  Unless
    <literal>discard</literal> is true, it is also called for each completed
    container, with a table as the value.  Numbers become Lua numbers unless
    <literal>pg_numeric</literal> is true, which leaves them as numeric
    datums; with <literal>norecurse</literal>, nested arrays and objects are
    returned as <type>jsonb</type> datums.
   </para>

   <para>
    To make a <type>jsonb</type> from a Lua value, use
    <literal>pgtype.jsonb(<replaceable>value</replaceable>, <replaceable>options</replaceable>)</literal>.
    A function returning <type>jsonb</type> can return the options table as a
    second value.  The options are:
<programlisting>
{ map = function(val) ... end, null = v, empty_object = bool,
  array_thresh = n, array_frac = n }
</programlisting>
    A <firstterm>collection</firstterm> is a table or a value with a
    <literal>__pairs</literal> metamethod.  Conversion works as follows:
    <itemizedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       A numeric datum becomes a JSON number, and any other datum is
       converted as <function>to_jsonb</function> would convert it, which
       needs <literal>EXECUTE</literal> privilege on that function.
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       A table marked as an object or array (see below) becomes one.  An
       empty collection becomes an empty array, or an empty object if
       <literal>empty_object</literal> is true.
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Any other collection whose keys are all positive integers becomes an
       array, unless that would insert more than
       <literal>array_thresh</literal> (default 1000) nulls at the start, or
       would make the array more than <literal>array_frac</literal>
       (default 1000) times as long as the number of keys.  Otherwise it
       becomes an object, if all keys are strings, numbers or values with a
       <literal>__tostring</literal> metamethod.
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       A value raw-equal to <literal>null</literal> is a JSON null; strings,
       numbers and booleans are the corresponding JSON values; other values with
       <literal>__tostring</literal> become strings.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </itemizedlist>
    The map function, if any, is called for every value including
    collections, before it is converted, but not for keys.  Tables made from
    <type>jsonb</type> values are marked as objects or arrays, so converting
    them back keeps the structure.  Lua tables cannot hold
    <literal>nil</literal>, so give the same unique <literal>null</literal> value
    in both directions if JSON nulls must survive, and
    <literal>pg_numeric</literal> if numbers must keep full precision:
<programlisting>
CREATE FUNCTION add_key(val jsonb) RETURNS jsonb AS $$
    local nullval = {}
    local t = val{ null = nullval, pg_numeric = true }
    t.added = { { foo = 1 }, { bar = 2 } }
    return t, { null = nullval }
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;
</programlisting>
   </para>

   <para>
    The module <literal>pl_lua.jsonb</literal> provides:
    <variablelist>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><function>is_object(t)</function>, <function>is_array(t)</function></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        True if the table is marked as an object (or array), false if it is
        marked as the other, and nothing if it is not marked.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><function>set_as_object(t)</function>, <function>set_as_array(t)</function>, <function>set_as_unknown(t)</function></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        Mark the table as an object or array, or remove the mark, and return
        the table.  The table must not have any other metatable.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><function>pairs(v)</function>, <function>ipairs(v)</function></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        Iterate over a <type>jsonb</type> value.  <function>ipairs</function>
        numbers array elements from 0 and does not stop at a null.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><function>type(v [, lax])</function></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        The JSON type of a <type>jsonb</type> value: <literal>array</literal>,
        <literal>object</literal>, <literal>null</literal>,
        <literal>number</literal>, <literal>string</literal> or
        <literal>boolean</literal>.  If <literal>lax</literal> is true, plain Lua
        values and numerics are classified too; otherwise anything else gives
        <literal>nil</literal>.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-datetime">
   <title>Date and Time Values</title>

   <para>
    Values of the types <type>timestamp with time zone</type>,
    <type>timestamp</type>, <type>date</type>, <type>time</type>,
    <type>time with time zone</type> and <type>interval</type> are datum
    objects.  Any field that the SQL <function>extract</function> function
    accepts (see <xref linkend="functions-datetime-extract"/>) can be read as a
    field of the object, such as <literal>d.year</literal>, <literal>d.dow</literal>
    or <literal>d.epoch</literal>.  There are also <literal>isoweek</literal>
    (the same as <literal>week</literal>), <literal>epoch_msec</literal> and
    <literal>epoch_usec</literal>.  The method <literal>d:as_table()</literal>
    returns all the fields as a table; for a <type>timestamp with time zone</type>,
    <literal>d:as_table(<replaceable>zone</replaceable>)</literal> gives the
    fields in the given time zone (a full name, not an abbreviation, or a
    numeric offset) instead of the session's.
   </para>

   <para>
    These types are made from a table of fields much as the Lua function
    <function>os.time</function> does:
<programlisting>
pgtype.timestamptz({ year = 2026, month = 3, day = 8, hour = 12 })
</programlisting>
    The fields are <literal>year</literal>, <literal>month</literal>,
    <literal>day</literal>, <literal>hour</literal>, <literal>min</literal>,
    <literal>sec</literal> (which may have a fraction), <literal>msec</literal>
    and <literal>usec</literal>, or else <literal>epoch</literal>,
    <literal>epoch_msec</literal> or <literal>epoch_usec</literal>, and
    <literal>timezone</literal> (a zone name, an offset string such as
    <literal>+0100</literal>, a number of seconds, or <literal>true</literal> for
    the session zone) and <literal>isdst</literal> (used only to resolve
    times made ambiguous by a daylight saving change).  The table from
    <function>as_table</function> of a <type>timestamp with time zone</type> also has
    <literal>timezone_abbrev</literal>, which is ignored on input.
    Calendar and epoch fields cannot be mixed.  Out-of-range calendar fields are
    normalized, except for <type>interval</type>; <literal>msec</literal> and
    <literal>usec</literal> are applied last.  Each calendar field, and the whole
    seconds of <literal>sec</literal>, must fit in 32 bits.  An infinite value
    is accepted only with an epoch field, for <type>timestamp</type> and
    <type>timestamp with time zone</type>.
   </para>

   <para>
    The type decides how the fields are used:
    <variablelist>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><type>date</type></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        Calendar fields are used as given, and no zone is allowed.  An epoch
        gives the date containing that instant in the given zone, or UTC.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><type>timestamp with time zone</type></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        An epoch gives that instant, and no zone is allowed.  Calendar fields
        are read in the given zone, or the session zone, and unspecified time
        fields are zero.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><type>timestamp</type></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        An epoch with a zone gives the local time in that zone at that instant;
        without one it is an offset from 1970-01-01 00:00:00.  Calendar fields
        are used as given, with no zone.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><type>time</type></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        An epoch is an offset from midnight, modulo one day.  Calendar time
        fields are used; date and zone fields are ignored.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><type>time with time zone</type></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        As for <type>time</type>, but the zone must be a numeric or string
        offset, which becomes the offset of the result.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><type>interval</type></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>The fields are added without normalization.</para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="pllua-spi">
  <title>Database Access</title>

  <para>
   Lua code uses the global table <literal>spi</literal>, which is also the
   module <literal>pl_lua.spi</literal>, to run queries.  A query run by a
   function that is not <literal>VOLATILE</literal> is read-only and sees the
   snapshot of the calling query; other queries take a new snapshot each.
   <literal>spi.readonly()</literal> tells which applies.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="pllua-spi-queries">
   <title>Running Queries</title>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>spi.execute(<replaceable>query</replaceable> [, <replaceable>arg</replaceable>, ...])</literal></term>
     <term><literal>spi.execute_count(<replaceable>query</replaceable>, <replaceable>maxrows</replaceable> [, <replaceable>arg</replaceable>, ...])</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Run the query with the arguments as <literal>$1</literal>,
       <literal>$2</literal>, and so on.  A query that returns rows gives a table
       (possibly empty) of rows, each a composite datum indexed by column name;
       any other query gives the number of rows it processed.
       <function>execute_count</function> fetches at most
       <replaceable>maxrows</replaceable> rows (0 or <literal>nil</literal> means
       all).
<programlisting>
for i, row in ipairs(spi.execute("SELECT relname FROM pg_class WHERE relkind = $1", "r")) do
    print(row.relname)
end
</programlisting>
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>spi.rows(<replaceable>query</replaceable> [, <replaceable>arg</replaceable>, ...])</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Returns an iterator over the rows, which are fetched from a cursor in
       batches rather than all at once.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>spi.prepare(<replaceable>query</replaceable> [, <replaceable>argtypes</replaceable> [, <replaceable>options</replaceable>]])</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Prepares the query and returns a statement object.
       <replaceable>argtypes</replaceable> is a table of type names or type
       objects; parameters without one get their types from the query, as in
       <command>PREPARE</command>.  The same inference is used for queries run
       directly with <function>spi.execute</function> and the like, except that
       a parameter for which a datum object is passed has the type of that
       object, as the parameter of a typed value would, unless it is given
       another type by the query itself.  Other Lua values do not determine a
       type: a string is an <type>unknown</type>-type literal, and a number or
       boolean not constrained by the query is passed in its text form.  Use
       <function>spi.prepare</function> with explicit types when a parameter
       must have a particular type.  The options table can contain
       <literal>scroll</literal>, <literal>no_scroll</literal>,
       <literal>hold</literal>, <literal>fast_start</literal>,
       <literal>custom_plan</literal> and <literal>generic_plan</literal> (all
       booleans), and <literal>fetch_count</literal>, the number of rows an
       iterator fetches at a time (default 50).  Statement objects are freed by
       garbage collection and can be kept for the life of the session.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>spi.findcursor(<replaceable>name</replaceable>)</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Returns a cursor object for the open cursor of that name, not owned by
       the object, or <literal>nil</literal> if there is none.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>spi.newcursor([<replaceable>name</replaceable>])</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Returns the cursor object for the open cursor of that name if there is
       one; otherwise a new unopened cursor object that will use the name,
       if given, when it is opened.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>

   <para>
    A statement object <replaceable>s</replaceable> provides:
    <literal><replaceable>s</replaceable>:execute([arg, ...])</literal> (also
    written <literal><replaceable>s</replaceable>(arg, ...)</literal>),
    <literal>:execute_count(maxrows [, arg, ...])</literal> and
    <literal>:rows([arg, ...])</literal>, which work like the functions
    above; <literal>:getcursor([arg, ...])</literal>, which opens and returns
    an owned cursor with a system-chosen name; and
    <literal>:numargs()</literal> and <literal>:argtype(n)</literal>.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-spi-cursors">
   <title>Cursors</title>

   <para>
    A cursor object <replaceable>c</replaceable> provides:
    <variablelist>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal><replaceable>c</replaceable>:open(<replaceable>query-or-statement</replaceable> [, arg, ...])</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        Opens the cursor, which must not already be open, and returns it,
        owned.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal><replaceable>c</replaceable>:fetch([n [, direction]])</literal>, <literal><replaceable>c</replaceable>:move([n [, direction]])</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        Fetch rows as a table, as <function>spi.execute</function> does, or
        only move.  The direction is <literal>forward</literal> or
        <literal>next</literal> (the default), <literal>backward</literal> or
        <literal>prior</literal>, <literal>absolute</literal> or
        <literal>relative</literal>; <replaceable>n</replaceable> defaults
        to 1.  <literal>c:move(0, 'absolute')</literal> positions the cursor
        before the first row.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal><replaceable>c</replaceable>:rows()</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>Returns an iterator over the remaining rows.</para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal>:isopen()</literal>, <literal>:close()</literal>, <literal>:name()</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        Report whether the cursor is open, close it, and give its name.
        The object notices when its cursor is closed some other way, such as
        at the end of the transaction.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal>:isowned()</literal>, <literal>:own()</literal>, <literal>:disown()</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        Report or change ownership.  An owned cursor is closed when its object
        is garbage collected, which suits a cursor used only inside a function;
        a cursor handed back to the caller must be disowned.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
    There is at most one cursor object for an open portal.  A
    <type>refcursor</type> argument or result is converted to or from a
    cursor object automatically:
<programlisting>
CREATE FUNCTION open_objects() RETURNS refcursor AS $$
    local c = spi.newcursor('objcur')
    c:open('SELECT * FROM objects ORDER BY id')
    return c:disown()
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;
</programlisting>
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-spi-quote">
   <title>Quoting</title>

   <para>
    These work like the SQL functions of the same names and raise an error
    for a string containing a zero byte:
    <literal>spi.quote_ident(s)</literal>, <literal>spi.quote_literal(s)</literal>
    (numbers are converted to strings first) and
    <literal>spi.quote_nullable(v)</literal>, which gives
    <literal>NULL</literal> for <literal>nil</literal> or no argument.
    Passing values as query parameters is preferable to quoting them.
<programlisting>
spi.execute('ALTER TABLE ' .. spi.quote_ident(tab) .. ' RENAME TO ' .. spi.quote_ident(new))
</programlisting>
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-spi-helpers">
   <title>Other Convenience Functions</title>

   <para>
    These functions of the <literal>spi</literal> module are written in Lua,
    and make common jobs shorter:
    <variablelist>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal>spi.return_query(<replaceable>query</replaceable> [, <replaceable>arg</replaceable>, ...])</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        For use in a set-returning function: runs the query and yields each of
        its rows as a row of the function's result, so that
        <literal>spi.return_query("SELECT ...")</literal> is the same as a loop
        over <function>spi.rows</function> that yields every row.  If the
        function returns a composite type (or has several output columns), the
        columns of the query are matched to those of the result by position;
        otherwise the query must have one column.  Other rows can be yielded
        before and after, and the function stops early when the caller does.
        Elsewhere it raises an error.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal>spi.encode_typed_literal(<replaceable>value</replaceable>, <replaceable>typename</replaceable>)</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        The text form of <replaceable>value</replaceable> as a value of the
        named type, without quotes, which is the same as
        <literal>tostring(pgtype[<replaceable>typename</replaceable>](<replaceable>value</replaceable>))</literal>
        (<literal>nil</literal> gives <literal>nil</literal>).  Use
        <function>spi.quote_literal</function> to turn it into a SQL literal.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal>spi.encode_array_literal(<replaceable>table</replaceable> [, <replaceable>delimiter</replaceable>])</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        The text form of an array, such as <literal>{1,"a b",NULL}</literal>,
        made from a table of values, or of tables for more dimensions.
        <literal>nil</literal> in the sequence is an SQL null, and values are
        converted with <function>tostring</function> and quoted as needed.  The
        delimiter is a comma unless given.  The length of the sequence is the
        field <literal>n</literal> of the table if it has one, as one made by
        <function>table.pack</function> does, which is the way to have nulls
        in it, and otherwise the length given by the <literal>#</literal>
        operator, which is not reliable for a table with holes.  The same
        applies to <function>spi.encode_array_constructor</function>.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal>spi.encode_array_constructor(<replaceable>table</replaceable>)</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        An <literal>ARRAY[...]</literal> expression with each element as a quoted
        literal, such as <literal>ARRAY['1','a b',NULL]</literal>, for use in
        dynamically built SQL.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
     <varlistentry>
      <term><literal>spi.looks_like_number(<replaceable>value</replaceable>)</literal></term>
      <listitem>
       <para>
        True for a Lua number, a <type>numeric</type> datum, and a string that is
        written as a decimal number, possibly with a sign, fraction, exponent
        and surrounding white space, or is <literal>Inf</literal>,
        <literal>Infinity</literal> or <literal>NaN</literal>.  Unlike
        <function>tonumber</function> it is false for hexadecimal strings, and
        never raises an error.
       </para>
      </listitem>
     </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-spi-transactions">
   <title>Transaction Control</title>

   <para>
    A procedure or <command>DO</command> block called from the top level can
    end transactions with <literal>spi.commit()</literal> and
    <literal>spi.rollback()</literal>; a new transaction starts automatically.
    The SQL commands <command>COMMIT</command> and <command>ROLLBACK</command>
    cannot be run through <literal>spi.execute</literal>.
    <literal>spi.is_atomic()</literal> is false when transaction control is
    possible, which it is not inside <function>pcall</function>, whose body runs
    in a subtransaction.  Cursors from statements prepared without the
    <literal>hold</literal> option are closed when the transaction ends.
<programlisting>
CREATE PROCEDURE keep_evens() AS $$
    for i = 0, 9 do
        spi.execute("INSERT INTO t(a) VALUES ($1)", i)
        if i % 2 == 0 then spi.commit() else spi.rollback() end
    end
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;
</programlisting>
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="pllua-errors">
  <title>Messages and Errors</title>

  <sect2 id="pllua-errors-raise">
   <title>Reporting Messages</title>

   <para>
    The global function <function>print</function> sends its arguments,
    separated by tabs, to the client as an <literal>INFO</literal> message
    (to the server log, as <literal>LOG</literal>, when called from an
    initialization setting).  Other messages and errors are raised with
    these functions, which are in the <literal>spi</literal> table and in the
    module <literal>pl_lua.elog</literal>:
<programlisting>
spi.elog(level, message)
spi.elog(level, sqlstate, message [, detail [, hint]])
spi.elog(level, { sqlstate = ..., message = ..., detail = ..., hint = ...,
                  table = ..., column = ..., datatype = ..., constraint = ...,
                  schema = ... })
spi.debug(...)   spi.log(...)   spi.info(...)
spi.notice(...)  spi.warning(...)  spi.error(...)
</programlisting>
    The level is one of <literal>debug</literal>, <literal>log</literal>,
    <literal>info</literal>, <literal>notice</literal>,
    <literal>warning</literal> or <literal>error</literal>; the other functions
    each use the level of their name.  The <literal>sqlstate</literal> is a
    five-character code or a condition name from <xref linkend="errcodes-appendix"/>.
    Whether a message reaches the client or the log depends on
    <xref linkend="guc-client-min-messages"/> and <xref linkend="guc-log-min-messages"/>.
    An error without a SQLSTATE uses <literal>XX000</literal>.
<programlisting>
spi.error('invalid_parameter_value', 'value out of range', 'value was ' .. v)
</programlisting>
    An error aborts the transaction or subtransaction unless
    <function>pcall</function> catches it.  The standard Lua
    <function>error</function> function also works; an uncaught Lua error is
    reported with the Lua message and SQLSTATE <literal>38000</literal>
    (<literal>external_routine_exception</literal>), but an error object
    that <function>error</function> rethrows keeps its own SQLSTATE.
    Message text, including the arguments of <function>print</function> and
    <function>warn</function>, Lua error messages, and every field passed to
    these functions, must be valid in the database encoding; if it is not,
    an error with SQLSTATE <literal>22021</literal> is raised instead.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-errors-catch">
   <title>Catching Errors</title>

   <para>
    In PL/Lua, <function>pcall</function> and <function>xpcall</function> run
    the called function in a subtransaction, so they can catch SQL errors as
    well as Lua errors.  On an error the subtransaction is rolled back,
    undoing the database changes the function made, and the error is
    returned as usual; otherwise the subtransaction is committed.
    <function>xpcall</function> ends the subtransaction before it calls the
    message handler.  Query cancellation and other operator interventions
    cannot be caught.
<programlisting>
CREATE FUNCTION try_insert(k integer, v text) RETURNS boolean AS $$
    local ok, err = pcall(spi.execute, "INSERT INTO objects VALUES ($1, $2)", k, v)
    if not ok then
        if err.errcode == 'unique_violation' then return false end
        error(err)
    end
    return true
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;
</programlisting>
    <function>lpcall</function> is a lighter version of
    <function>pcall</function> that starts no subtransaction.  It catches
    Lua errors but passes SQL errors on at once, so it is useful mainly for
    code that does not use the database.  Likewise
    <function>coroutine.resume</function> passes SQL errors on instead of
    returning them.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="pllua-errors-objects">
   <title>Error Objects</title>

   <para>
    A SQL error caught in Lua code is an error object, not a string;
    <function>tostring</function> gives <literal>ERROR:</literal>, the SQLSTATE and
    the message, followed by any detail and hint.  The fields
    <literal>sqlstate</literal> (the five-character code),
    <literal>errcode</literal> (the condition name, or the code if it has
    none), <literal>category</literal> (the name of the error class, or the class
    code), <literal>severity</literal> (always <literal>error</literal>) and
    <literal>message</literal> are always present.  These are present when the
    error supplied them: <literal>detail</literal>, <literal>hint</literal>,
    <literal>context</literal>, <literal>schema</literal>, <literal>table</literal>,
    <literal>column</literal>, <literal>datatype</literal>,
    <literal>constraint</literal>, <literal>position</literal> and
    <literal>internal_position</literal> (integers),
    <literal>internal_query</literal>, <literal>message_id</literal> (the
    untranslated message), <literal>pg_source_file</literal>,
    <literal>pg_source_line</literal> (an integer) and
    <literal>pg_source_function</literal>.
   </para>

   <para>
    The module <literal>pl_lua.error</literal> has the functions
    <function>type</function>, <function>errcode</function> and
    <function>category</function>.  <literal>type(e)</literal> is
    <literal>error</literal> for an error object; the other two return the
    field of that name.  All return nothing for any other value, which makes
    it easy to tell SQL errors from ordinary Lua errors.
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="pllua-triggers">
  <title>Trigger Functions</title>

  <indexterm zone="pllua-triggers">
   <primary>trigger</primary>
   <secondary>in PL/Lua</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   A trigger function has no declared arguments and returns
   <type>trigger</type>.  Its Lua parameters are <literal>(trigger, old, new,
   ...)</literal>: the trigger object described below, the old and new rows
   (or <literal>nil</literal> where there are none), and the arguments given
   in <command>CREATE TRIGGER</command>, as strings.  The trigger object has
   these read-only fields, except that <literal>row</literal> can be assigned
   to:
   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>new</literal>, <literal>old</literal></term>
     <listitem><para>The same as the parameters.</para></listitem>
    </varlistentry>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>row</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       The row the trigger returns by default: <literal>new</literal> for
       inserts and updates, <literal>old</literal> for deletes.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>name</literal>, <literal>when</literal>, <literal>operation</literal> (or <literal>op</literal>), <literal>level</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       The trigger name; <literal>before</literal>, <literal>after</literal>
       or <literal>instead</literal>; <literal>insert</literal>,
       <literal>update</literal>, <literal>delete</literal> or
       <literal>truncate</literal>; <literal>row</literal> or
       <literal>statement</literal>.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>relation</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       A table with <literal>name</literal>, <literal>namespace</literal>,
       <literal>oid</literal> and <literal>attributes</literal>, which maps
       column names to attribute numbers.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>
   The columns of <literal>old</literal> and <literal>new</literal> can be
   assigned to.  The return value matters only for row-level
   <literal>BEFORE</literal> and <literal>INSTEAD OF</literal> triggers, where
   the function can:
   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      return no value at all, to go ahead using <literal>trigger.row</literal>
      with any changes made to it;
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      return a row, or a table indexed by column name, to be converted to
      the table's row type and used instead;
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      return <literal>nil</literal>, or set <literal>trigger.row</literal> to
      <literal>nil</literal>, to skip the operation for this row.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>
<programlisting>
CREATE FUNCTION set_total() RETURNS trigger AS $$
    new.total_cost = new.price * new.qty
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;

CREATE TRIGGER orders_total BEFORE INSERT OR UPDATE ON orders
    FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION set_total();
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <sect2 id="pllua-event-triggers">
   <title>Event Trigger Functions</title>

   <para>
    An event trigger function (see <xref linkend="event-triggers"/>) returns
    <type>event_trigger</type> and has the one parameter
    <literal>trigger</literal>, with the fields <literal>event</literal> and
    <literal>tag</literal>.
<programlisting>
CREATE FUNCTION log_ddl() RETURNS event_trigger AS $$
    print(trigger.event, trigger.tag)
$$ LANGUAGE pl_lua;
</programlisting>
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="pllua-sandbox">
  <title>The Trusted Sandbox</title>

  <para>
   Functions and blocks in the trusted language <literal>pl_lua</literal>
   run with a sandbox as their global environment, separate from the real
   one.  The sandbox contains only:
   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      the basic functions <function>assert</function>,
      <function>collectgarbage</function>, <function>error</function>,
      <function>getmetatable</function>, <function>ipairs</function>,
      <function>next</function>, <function>pairs</function>,
      <function>rawequal</function>, <function>rawlen</function>,
      <function>rawget</function>, <function>rawset</function>,
      <function>select</function>, <function>setmetatable</function>,
      <function>tonumber</function>, <function>tostring</function>,
      <function>type</function> and <function>warn</function>, and the
      PL/Lua versions of <function>print</function>,
      <function>pcall</function>, <function>xpcall</function> and
      <function>lpcall</function>;
     </para>
     <para>
      <function>setmetatable</function> does not accept a metatable that has
      a <literal>__gc</literal> field, so trusted code cannot define
      finalizers.  The garbage collector can start at any allocation, including
      those made by PL/Lua's own code while it works with the database, and a
      finalizer would run user code at such a point.  The
      <literal>__close</literal> metamethod, for to-be-closed variables, is
      available for cleanup.  The untrusted language has no such restriction;
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      a <function>load</function> that accepts only text chunks (loading
      precompiled chunks is unsafe) and runs them in the sandbox unless given
      an environment;
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      copies of the libraries <literal>coroutine</literal>,
      <literal>math</literal>, <literal>string</literal>,
      <literal>table</literal> and <literal>utf8</literal>, except that there is no
      <function>string.dump</function>, which would let trusted code read
      the compiled form, including the constants, of any function it can call
      but did not write, such as one from a module an administrator has made
      available.  <function>string.dump</function> is also missing from the
      code in <varname>pl_lua.on_trusted_init</varname>, but is available in
      <literal>pl_luau</literal>;
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      an <literal>os</literal> library with only <function>clock</function>,
      <function>date</function>, <function>difftime</function> and
      <function>time</function>;
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      a restricted <literal>package</literal> and <function>require</function>,
      which load only modules that have been allowed (see below) or that
      sandbox code supplies through <literal>package.preload</literal> or
      <literal>package.searchers</literal>;
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      the PL/Lua modules <literal>pl_lua.spi</literal>,
      <literal>pl_lua.pgtype</literal>, <literal>pl_lua.elog</literal>,
      <literal>pl_lua.error</literal>, <literal>pl_lua.numeric</literal>,
      <literal>pl_lua.jsonb</literal> and <literal>pl_lua.time</literal>, and
      (if <xref linkend="guc-pl-lua-install-globals"/> is on) the global tables
      <literal>spi</literal> and <literal>pgtype</literal>;
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      the globals <literal>_VERSION</literal>, <literal>_PLVERSION</literal>,
      <literal>_PLREVISION</literal>, <literal>_PG_VERSION</literal>,
      <literal>_PG_VERSION_NUM</literal>, <literal>_PL_IDENT</literal>,
      <literal>_PL_LOAD_TIME</literal>, and <literal>_G</literal>, which
      is the sandbox.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>
   The module <literal>pl_lua.paths</literal>, available only in
   <literal>pl_luau</literal> and in the initialization settings, has the
   functions <function>bin</function>, <function>lib</function>,
   <function>pkglib</function> (and <function>libdir</function>, the directory
   that <literal>$libdir</literal> means), <function>share</function>,
   <function>locale</function>, <function>doc</function>,
   <function>etc</function>, <function>html</function>,
   <function>include</function>, <function>includeserver</function>,
   <function>man</function> and <function>pkginclude</function>, which return
   the corresponding installation directories.  Some might not exist.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="pllua-sandbox-modules">
   <title>Allowing More Modules</title>

   <para>
    The initialization settings <varname>pl_lua.on_init</varname> and
    <varname>pl_lua.on_trusted_init</varname> run outside the sandbox and can
    use the module <literal>pl_lua.trusted</literal> to make other modules
    available inside it.  It is the global <literal>trusted</literal> in
    <varname>pl_lua.on_trusted_init</varname> (if
    <varname>pl_lua.install_globals</varname> is on), and must be fetched with
    <literal>require 'pl_lua.trusted'</literal> in
    <varname>pl_lua.on_init</varname>.  For example,
<programlisting>
pl_lua.on_trusted_init = 'trusted.allow{"lpeg", "re"}'
</programlisting>
    makes the modules <literal>lpeg</literal> and <literal>re</literal>
    available to <function>require</function> in the sandbox.
   </para>

   <warning>
    <para>
     The administrator must judge whether a module is safe to expose to
     trusted code.  Many modules, written in Lua or in C, can be used to break
     security or crash the server.  Warning signs are input/output or network
     facilities; code that assumes the caller's <literal>_G</literal> or
     <literal>_ENV</literal> is its own, or exposes its own
     <literal>_G</literal>; and C code that uses <function>lua_pcall</function>
     or <function>lua_resume</function> to call code that might raise an SQL
     error.  For anything more complex than <literal>lpeg</literal>, consider
     whether the untrusted language is a better fit.
    </para>
   </warning>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>trusted.allow(<replaceable>module</replaceable> [, <replaceable>newname</replaceable> [, <replaceable>mode</replaceable> [, <replaceable>global</replaceable> [, <replaceable>preload</replaceable>]]]])</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Makes the module available in the sandbox as
       <literal>require 'newname'</literal> (by default under its own name).
       It is not loaded until first required, unless
       <replaceable>global</replaceable> or <replaceable>preload</replaceable>
       is true.  If <replaceable>global</replaceable> is true or a string, the
       module is also stored in a sandbox global variable, named by the
       string or else by <replaceable>newname</replaceable>.  Preloading
       is useful for loading modules before the server forks when interpreters
       are prebuilt (see <xref linkend="pllua-config"/>).  The
       <replaceable>mode</replaceable> says how the sandbox sees the module:
       <literal>direct</literal> gives the module table itself,
       <literal>copy</literal> a recursive copy without metatables,
       <literal>scopy</literal> a shallow copy, <literal>proxy</literal> (the
       default) a proxy table whose <literal>__index</literal> is the module
       with nested tables proxied likewise, and <literal>sproxy</literal> a
       proxy without the recursion.  All behave like <literal>direct</literal>
       if the module is not a table.  A function can also be given, which
       receives the module and returns what to expose.  A proxy protects the
       module itself from change but is not a security boundary: tables returned
       by the module's functions can still be changed.  Neither proxy nor copy
       works for every module; those that use tables as keys or unique
       identifiers, or rely on metatables other than <literal>__call</literal>,
       are likely to fail.  If <replaceable>module</replaceable> is a table, it
       is a list whose elements are module names or tables
       <literal>{ module, newname, mode, global, preload }</literal>, with the
       other arguments filling in missing values.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>trusted.require(<replaceable>module</replaceable> [, <replaceable>newname</replaceable> [, <replaceable>mode</replaceable>]])</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       The same as <literal>trusted.allow(module, newname, mode, true)</literal>:
       the module is loaded at once and stored as a global in the sandbox.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>trusted.remove(<replaceable>newname</replaceable> [, <replaceable>global</replaceable>])</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Undoes <function>trusted.allow</function>.  If
       <replaceable>global</replaceable> is a string, the sandbox global of that
       name is removed too; if it is true, the one named
       <replaceable>newname</replaceable> is.  This can also remove standard
       modules, for example <literal>trusted.remove('os', true)</literal>.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="pllua-config">
  <title>Configuration</title>

  <para>
   An interpreter is created on demand: the first time PL/Lua code runs in a
   session, and again for trusted code run as a role that has none yet.
   If <literal>pl_lua</literal> is listed in
   <xref linkend="guc-shared-preload-libraries"/>, interpreters are instead
   partly built in the postmaster and inherited by server processes, which
   saves start-up time in each session and lets modules loaded by
   <varname>pl_lua.on_init</varname> be shared.  (On platforms where server
   processes are started afresh rather than forked, such as Windows, this
   cannot work, and interpreters are always built on demand.)  Beware that an error in
   <varname>pl_lua.on_init</varname> then stops the server from starting.
   Creating an interpreter takes these steps:
   <orderedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The standard Lua libraries are loaded; <function>print</function>,
      <function>pcall</function>, <function>xpcall</function> and
      <function>coroutine.resume</function> are replaced by the PL/Lua versions
      and <function>lpcall</function> is added; the modules
      <literal>pl_lua.trusted</literal> and <literal>pl_lua.paths</literal> are loaded;
      and <varname>pl_lua.on_init</varname> is run.  This much can happen in
      the postmaster, so database access is not possible.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The other PL/Lua modules are loaded, the globals are set up, and, for a
      trusted interpreter, the sandbox is built.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>pl_lua.on_trusted_init</varname> or
      <varname>pl_lua.on_untrusted_init</varname> is run, outside the sandbox.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>pl_lua.on_common_init</varname> is run, inside the sandbox in a
      trusted interpreter.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </orderedlist>
   An error in any of them stops the creation of the interpreter and is
   reported as an error of the query that needed it.
  </para>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry id="guc-pl-lua-on-init" xreflabel="pl_lua.on_init">
    <term><varname>pl_lua.on_init</varname> (<type>string</type>)
    <indexterm><primary><varname>pl_lua.on_init</varname> configuration parameter</primary></indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Lua code run in step 1 above, before the interpreter is specialized
      as trusted or untrusted.  It can load modules, which will then be
      available to untrusted code, and can use
      <literal>require 'pl_lua.trusted'</literal> to make modules available to
      trusted code.  Only superusers and users with the appropriate
      <literal>SET</literal> privilege can change it.  Changing it in a session
      does not affect existing interpreters.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-pl-lua-on-trusted-init" xreflabel="pl_lua.on_trusted_init">
    <term><varname>pl_lua.on_trusted_init</varname> (<type>string</type>)
    <indexterm><primary><varname>pl_lua.on_trusted_init</varname> configuration parameter</primary></indexterm>
    </term>
    <term><varname>pl_lua.on_untrusted_init</varname> (<type>string</type>)
    <indexterm><primary><varname>pl_lua.on_untrusted_init</varname> configuration parameter</primary></indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Lua code run in step 3 for a trusted or untrusted interpreter.  Database
      access is possible.  The code runs outside the sandbox, so it can use
      <literal>trusted</literal> (see <xref linkend="pllua-sandbox-modules"/>), but
      like sandbox code it has no <function>string.dump</function>.  Only
      superusers and users with the appropriate <literal>SET</literal> privilege
      can change these.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-pl-lua-on-common-init" xreflabel="pl_lua.on_common_init">
    <term><varname>pl_lua.on_common_init</varname> (<type>string</type>)
    <indexterm><primary><varname>pl_lua.on_common_init</varname> configuration parameter</primary></indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Lua code run last for every interpreter (step 4), inside the sandbox
      in a trusted interpreter.  Only superusers and users with the
      appropriate <literal>SET</literal> privilege can change it.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-pl-lua-install-globals" xreflabel="pl_lua.install_globals">
    <term><varname>pl_lua.install_globals</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
    <indexterm><primary><varname>pl_lua.install_globals</varname> configuration parameter</primary></indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      When on (the default), the modules <literal>spi</literal> and
      <literal>pgtype</literal> are stored in global variables of those names,
      both in the real global environment and in the sandbox, and
      <literal>pl_lua.trusted</literal> is stored as the global
      <literal>trusted</literal> outside the sandbox.  When off, code must call
      <function>require</function>.  It takes effect when an interpreter is
      created.  Only superusers and users with the appropriate
      <literal>SET</literal> privilege can change it.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-pl-lua-strict" xreflabel="pl_lua.strict">
    <term><varname>pl_lua.strict</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
    <indexterm><primary><varname>pl_lua.strict</varname> configuration parameter</primary></indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      When on, reading a variable found in neither the function's own
      environment nor the global environment is an error instead of
      <literal>nil</literal>.  Assignments are not affected.  It applies to
      functions and blocks compiled after it is changed; functions already
      compiled in the session keep their behavior.  The default is off.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-pl-lua-check-for-interrupts" xreflabel="pl_lua.check_for_interrupts">
    <term><varname>pl_lua.check_for_interrupts</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
    <indexterm><primary><varname>pl_lua.check_for_interrupts</varname> configuration parameter</primary></indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      When on (the default), a Lua hook checks for query cancel requests and
      other interrupts while Lua code runs, so that a long-running function can
      be canceled.  It takes effect when an interpreter is created.  Only
      superusers and users with the appropriate <literal>SET</literal>
      privilege can change it.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-pl-lua-prebuilt-interpreters" xreflabel="pl_lua.prebuilt_interpreters">
    <term><varname>pl_lua.prebuilt_interpreters</varname> (<type>integer</type>)
    <indexterm><primary><varname>pl_lua.prebuilt_interpreters</varname> configuration parameter</primary></indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      When <literal>pl_lua</literal> is loaded through
      <varname>shared_preload_libraries</varname>, how many interpreters the
      postmaster builds, from 0 to 10; the default is 1.
      <varname>pl_lua.on_init</varname> runs separately in each.  More are
      always created on demand when these are used up, so building more than one
      helps only if most sessions use both languages, or trusted code as more
      than one role.  Changing the setting causes the prebuilt interpreters to
      be built again.  This can be set only in
      <filename>postgresql.conf</filename> or on the server command line.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-pl-lua-interpreter-reload-ident" xreflabel="pl_lua.interpreter_reload_ident">
    <term><varname>pl_lua.interpreter_reload_ident</varname> (<type>string</type>)
    <indexterm><primary><varname>pl_lua.interpreter_reload_ident</varname> configuration parameter</primary></indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      When <literal>pl_lua</literal> is loaded through
      <varname>shared_preload_libraries</varname>, changing this causes the
      prebuilt interpreters to be discarded and built again.  While it is not
      empty, changing <varname>pl_lua.on_init</varname> in the configuration
      file has the same effect; otherwise the interpreters are rebuilt only when
      the server restarts.  The value is stored in new interpreters as the global
      <literal>_PL_IDENT</literal>; when it changes, interpreters in running
      sessions get the new value in <literal>_PL_IDENT_NEW</literal> before their
      next use.  This can be set only in <filename>postgresql.conf</filename> or
      on the server command line.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-pl-lua-extra-gc-multiplier" xreflabel="pl_lua.extra_gc_multiplier">
    <term><varname>pl_lua.extra_gc_multiplier</varname> (<type>floating point</type>)
    <indexterm><primary><varname>pl_lua.extra_gc_multiplier</varname> configuration parameter</primary></indexterm>
    </term>
    <term><varname>pl_lua.extra_gc_threshold</varname> (<type>floating point</type>)
    <indexterm><primary><varname>pl_lua.extra_gc_threshold</varname> configuration parameter</primary></indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The Lua garbage collector does not know about memory that is kept
      outside Lua for the objects that refer to it, namely the plans of prepared
      statements, so it might not run as often as it should in a function that
      makes many of them.  (The values of datum objects are held in Lua's own
      memory, and are counted by the collector.)  If <varname>pl_lua.extra_gc_multiplier</varname> is above zero,
      PL/Lua estimates that memory, and once it reaches
      <varname>pl_lua.extra_gc_threshold</varname> kilobytes it does an
      incremental collection step, proportional to the amount times the
      multiplier, before returning from Lua code.  The largest multiplier,
      1000000, does a full collection instead.  Both default to 0, which
      means no extra collection.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="pllua-hstore">
  <title>The <type>hstore</type> Transforms</title>

  <para>
   The extensions <literal>hstore_pl_lua</literal> (for
   <literal>pl_lua</literal>) and <literal>hstore_pl_luau</literal> (for
   <literal>pl_luau</literal>) provide transforms for the
   <type>hstore</type> type.  A function declared with
   <literal>TRANSFORM FOR TYPE hstore</literal> receives <type>hstore</type>
   values as Lua tables and converts returned tables to <type>hstore</type>.
   Keys with null values are left out of the table, because Lua tables cannot
   hold <literal>nil</literal>.  When converting a table to
   <type>hstore</type>, keys must be strings or numbers and values must be
   strings, numbers, booleans or objects with a <literal>__tostring</literal>
   method; all are converted to text.
  </para>
 </sect1>
</chapter>
